  "query_preview": "..\nTonight: A strong 130 kt zonal jet aimed at Washington and \nNorth Idaho combined with a continued moist air mass with \nPWAT's 100-150 percent of normal will lead to orographically \ndriven upslope showers into the Cascades and Idaho Panhandle \ntonight while a strong rain shadow keeps the majority of Central\nand Eastern Washington dry. Snow showers will continue at\nWashington Pass this evening, tapering off overnight with a \nWinter Storm Warning in effect through 4 AM PST Friday. The mild\nair mass combined with breezy winds will keep low temperatures \non the mild side with readings mainly in the mid 30s to low 40s.\n\nFriday/Friday Night: A quick moving mid level wave will track\nacross the region with the bulk of the moisture aimed at the\nsouthern Columbia Basin, Palouse, Blue Mountains, LC Valley, and\nCentral Panhandle Mountains. Models are generating around a\nquarter to half inch of precipitation for these areas with snow\nlevels starting off between 4500-5000 feet during the day \nFriday, falling to 3500-4000 feet Friday Night. The NBM is \ncarrying a 70 percent chance for Lookout Pass to receive 1 inch \nor more of snow, with a 30 percent chance for Stevens Pass. \nRain may change to snow towards the tail end of the event for\nthe Camas Prairie with light accumulations. Further north \nprecipitation amounts fall off with up to a tenth of an inch for\nthe Upper Basin into the Spokane and Coeur d'Alene areas.\n\nSaturday through Monday: A brief area of high pressure moves \nover the Inland NW Saturday for dry weather except for a chance \nfor lingering light precipitation over the Idaho Panhandle. With\nall the boundary layer moisture in place there is likely to be \nstratus and fog blanketing the region. On Sunday the next system\napproaches. Light isentropic ascent and increasing moisture \nfrom the southwest has the potential to produce light \nprecipitation during the day with snow levels near the valley \nfloors initially along the East Slopes of the Cascades and \nnorthern valleys while snow levels rise by Sunday afternoon to \n4000-6000 feet south of Interstate 90. Deeper moisture pushes in\nSunday Night into early Monday as precipitable water values \nincrease to 200 percent of normal with snow levels rising to \naround 3000 feet along the Canadian border. Any valley \naccumulations are expected to be light with NBM carrying a 20 \npercent or less chance of 1 inch or more of valley snow \naccumulations. Light to moderate snow in the mountains along the\nEast Slopes of the Cascades into the Okanogan Highlands will \nlikely lead to a period of winter travel in the passes including\nWashington, Stevens, Blewett, Loup Loup, and Sherman.\n\nTuesday through Thursday: The region enters a drier period as a\nsplit flow pattern develops with systems weakening and heading\nsouth. With all the boundary layer moisture around areas of fog\nand low clouds are likely each day. Temperatures are forecast to\nbe close to seasonal normals with highs in the mid 30s to low\n40s and lows mainly in the upper 20s to mid 30s. JW",
